WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.562
A transaction groups related database operations
into one controlled unit of work

00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:10.150
BEGIN starts the transaction After BEGIN we can run
one or more

00:00:10.175 --> 00:00:15.875
statements COMMIT keeps the changes ROLLBACK
discards the changes made

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:20.488
since the transaction began The example on this
slide is designed for

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:26.000
safe practice First BEGIN starts a transaction Next
we

00:00:26.025 --> 00:00:29.338
update the age for MRN one zero zero three and use
RETURNING

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:34.900
to inspect the changed row Then ROLLBACK undoes that
update Finally

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.162
a SELECT checks the patient again When you run the
full example

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:43.338
pay attention to the two ages you see RETURNING
should show the

00:00:43.362 --> 00:00:48.938
temporary updated value inside the transaction The
final SELECT after ROLLBACK

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:53.450
should show the original value again This is a
powerful learning pattern

00:00:53.475 --> 00:00:57.838
because it lets you practice data-changing SQL
without permanently keeping the change

00:00:58.712 --> 00:01:03.400
In real applications transactions are also used when
several changes belong together

00:01:04.287 --> 00:01:08.788
For example if one operation succeeds but another
related operation fails we

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:11.525
may want to undo the whole unit rather than leave
the database

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:16.112
in a partial state For now remember the three words
BEGIN starts

00:01:16.262 --> 00:01:18.162
COMMIT keeps and ROLLBACK undoes
